  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   We will represent the function h(x) as a weighted combination of sine and cosine basis functions of different frequencies. This allows us to capture both periodic structures and spatial variations. The optimization will simultaneously learn the weightings for these basis functions, effectively decoding the optimal pattern without explicitly searching over high-dimensional function spaces.\n\n   The key insight is that minimal overlap solutions tend to exhibit high-frequency oscillatory behavior, which can be approximated using few Fourier modes. By using a base of orthogonal basis functions, we enable efficient optimization in a manageable low-dimensional space.\n\n   This layered approach (basis decomposition + gradient-based optimization) offers two main advantages over direct sample-based methods:\n   - Reduced dimensionality: Instead of optimizing over hundreds of independent variables, we're working with tens of weights + a weighting function\n   - Structure preservation: The Fourier-like basis naturally incorporates periodic components that empirically minimize cross-correlations\n\n   However, this approach has limitations:\n   - The accuracy depends strongly on the chosen basis functions\n   - The projection step must preserve both function value bounds and integral constraint\n   - We must dynamically adjust both basis weights and n-samples independently\n\n   The proposed method is expected to clear the 0.38 barrier by better modeling self-similarity across scales, unlike the provided parent which may be trapped near period-3 solutions.\n\n   Guide for implementation: This is a scientific guidance not binding specification. You may: \n   - Correct, adapt, simplify, extend, or replace proposed components when justified\n   - Use this approach as baseline for alternative formulations\n   - Modify hyperparameters without re-justification where optimization direction remains correct\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   We define h(x) as a decomposition over basis functions:\n\n     h(x) = c0 + sum_{j=1}^{m} c_j * \u03c6_j(x)\n\n   where \u03c6_j(x) are normalized orthogonal functions satisfying:\n      \u222b\u2080\u00b2 \u03c6_j(x)\u00b2 dx = 1\n      <\u03c6_i, \u03c6_j> = 0 for i\u2260j\n\n   Our basis will consist of:\n     - Linear basis: \u03c6\u2080(x) = x/2 (shifted to [0,2])\n     - Fourier basis: \u03c6_j(x) = cos(\u03c0(j-1)x/2) / \u222b[0,\u00b2]cos\u00b2(\u03c0(j-1)x/2)dx for j\u22651\n     - Delayed basis: \u03c6_j(x) = (x-j)/2 for normalization, adapted from echo models\n\n   Optimization minimizes the non-convex objective:\n      max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\n   subject to:\n      \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n      0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x \u2208 [0,2]\n\n   We'll use Legendre scaling with initial adjustment. The basis functions are orthogonal, enabling independent optimization of coefficients.\n\n   The integral constraint applies to the original function, not coefficient space. Projection involves:\n      Step 1: Compute h(x) = \u03a3c_j\u03c6_j(x) with current weights\n      Step 2: Clip values: h_clip(x) = max(0, min(1, h(x)))\n      Step 3: Normalize: scale all coefficients by s = 1 / (\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h_clip(x) dx)\n\n   This projection preserves:\n      - Function value bounds\n      - Integral constraint\n      - Relative amplitude relationships\n   via the coefficients.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Initialization:\n      - If parent exists, use its coefficients projected to new basis\n      - Else: randomize coefficients with rough scaling (norm ~ 0.5)\n\n   b. Core Loop (minimize overlap):\n     while time remaining and improvement:\n        1. Compute gradient of max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n           approximated by central differences over a coarse lattice of k\n        2. Update coefficients: c_i = c_i - lr * g_i + noise(scale)\n        3. Project current coefficients to ensure 0\u2264h\u22641 & \u222b=1\n           via weighted combination with target integral\n        4. Evaluate new overlap and record best\n\n   c. Basis Management:\n        - Periodically add higher-frequency basis functions as optimization stalls\n        - Monitor basis condition number and add/remove coefficients\n        - Implement exponential basis truncation by increasing j_max\n\n   d. Outer Loop (resolution boost):\n        - After convergence, double the number of basis functions\n        - Retrain the model with augmented representation\n        - Average near-optimal coefficients from coarse to fine resolution\n\n   e. Projection Steps:\n        - After each optimization step:\n            h(x) = \u03a3c_j\u03c6_j(x) \n            clip = max(0, min(1, h(x)))\n            S = \u222bclip(x) dx\n            Normalize clip(x) = clip(x) / S * target (target = 2/n_points)\n\n        - Resampling step:\n            If n_points changed, recompute h(x) on coarser/finer grid using long.  \n            basis interpolability\n\n   f. Evaluation:\n        max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx computed by:\n           full_conv = np.full(400, 1)\n           correlate h with [0,1] over overlapping regions, then max\n\n4. Search Choices\n   a. Basis parametrization:\n        Start: J=10 (3 Fourier, 5 polynomial, 2 delayed)\n        Expand: each doubling adds harmonics up to J(x) = floor(2.7*initial)\n        Polishing: add random harmonics (3 or 4) in a final refinement phase\n\n   b. Coefficient optimization:\n        Initial method: L-BFGS-B with box constraints [0,1]\n        Fallback: SLSQP with integral constraint included directly\n        Try quadratic penalty for bounds violation: P = 10*\u2211(max(0,abs(c_i)-0.5)\u00b2)\n\n   c. Projection Schedule:\n        Run every 10 optimization steps\n        Use exponential averaging: store weights from all best regions\n\n   d. Noise Strategy:\n        Gaussian noise 0-0.05 in coefficients except linear term\n        Cauchy noise with skew toward coefficient growth (sharp peaks)\n\n5. Compute Budget\n   - Initial J: 10 modes -> ~40 variables\n   - Time estimates per resolution: 200 seconds (1100 total)\n   - Optimal splits:\n        Phase1: Basis selection (20% budget)\n        Phase2: Coefficient optimization (50% budget)\n        Phase3: Resolution boosting (25% budget)\n        Phase4: Multi-resolution polishing (5% budget)\n\n   Monitor:\n   if evaluation exceeds 1h, switch to passive mode: repeated evaluations with decreasing lr\n\n6. Validation\n   1. Run initial projection checks to ensure integral matches \u22641e-10\n   2. Verify 0\u2264h\u22641 for all sampled points\n   3. Cross-check overlap value with:\n        C5 = (np.correlate_projected_basis(h, [0,1]))[0] * dx\n        where dx = (b-a)/n\n\n   Fallback:\n   if basis invalidation detected (projection matrix broken):\n        revert to last valid solution\n        reduce basis components until problem resolves\n\n   Output must satisfy:\n     allcoefficients \u2208 [0,1]\n     sum(c\u2080 + \u03a3c_j\u03c6_j(x))dx =1\n     max_k overlap <1/3 (empirical)\n\n   Return structure: (h_samples, evaluation_score, n_points)\n   where h_samples is the n_points-length vector obtained by projecting principal components",
